<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is it normal for my rabbit to thump every single night?</h3>
<p>Yes, this can be normal, especially if your rabbit is reacting to the same recurring trigger — a sound outside, a light cycle, or a nearby pet. Since rabbits are most alert around dawn and dusk and often stay active overnight, nightly thumping on its own is usually just a repeated alarm response rather than a sign of a worsening problem.</p>

<h3>Should I comfort my rabbit after it thumps?</h3>
<p>You can check on your rabbit calmly to see if something is actually wrong nearby, but there's no need to intervene every time. Rabbits recover from a single thump quickly on their own. If your rabbit seems genuinely rattled afterward (hiding, refusing to settle), a calm, quiet environment and giving them space usually helps more than handling them.</p>

<h3>Can I stop my rabbit from thumping at night?</h3>
<p>You can reduce the triggers — moving the cage away from exterior walls or windows, keeping other pets separated at night, and keeping the room's lighting and noise consistent — but you can't fully eliminate an instinctive alarm behavior, and you shouldn't try to. The goal is managing triggers, not suppressing a normal communication signal.</p>

<h3>Does thumping mean my rabbit is scared of me?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Thumping is usually directed at an external trigger (a sound, movement, or another animal), not at a specific person. If your rabbit consistently thumps specifically when you approach and also shows other fearful body language around you, that's worth addressing through calm, patient handling, but thumping alone isn't proof a rabbit is afraid of its owner.</p>

